<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sasak people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sasak</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sasak are the indigenous people of the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombok">Lombok</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, speaking an Austronesian language related to Balinese. Most are Muslim, and some, the Wetu Telu, combine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> with older customs. In the 18th and 19th centuries Balinese princes ruled Lombok, and a Sasak revolt in 1891 led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> intervention and conquest of the island in 1894.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sasak-people.jpg" alt="Sasak people" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fiyan Zaki · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pulih_Tuturan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Sasak live mainly on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombok">Lombok</a> in West Nusa Tenggara Province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, and number around 3.6 million, which is 85% of the island's population. They are related to the Balinese in language and in ancestry, and to other ethnic groups on the neighbouring island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumbawa">Sumbawa</a>. The Bayan people are a distinct part of the Sasak and are the oldest group on Lombok.</p><p>The name Sasak was first mentioned in the Pujungan Inscription of the 11th century, found in Tabanan Regency on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>. Its origin is uncertain. It may come from sak-sak, meaning boat or one by one, and the 14th-century Nagarakṛtāgama pairs Sasak with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombok">Lombok</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-javanese">Kawi</a> phrase Lombok Sasak Mirah Adhi. Local tradition derives it from sa'-saq, 'the one'. Another theory links it to the word for weaving, since the sound of the loom resembles sak-sak.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Little is known about early Sasak history. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombok">Lombok</a> was brought under direct rule by the Mahapatih Gajah Mada by the 14th century. Before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> became widespread the island was primarily <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>, and many ancient Hindu temples, among them Gunung Pengsong, Lingsar, Meru and Suranadi, still stand.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> reached the area around the 15th century, and the Sasak converted between the late 16th and early 17th centuries. This is attributed to Pangeran Prapen, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunan-giri">Raden Paku</a> (Sunan Giri), or alternatively to Sunan Giri himself and the Muslim Makassarese, who frequently mixed basic Islamic belief with animism and so created the Wetu Telu religion. Balinese kingdoms also conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombok">Lombok</a>, bringing a large Balinese population, about 300,000 people or 10 to 15 percent of the island's inhabitants, and strongly influencing Wetu Telu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Sasak language, Base Sasaq, is an Austronesian language of the Malayo-Sumbawan branch, closely related to the languages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>. It has several dialects, including Kutó-Kuté and Bayan-Sasak in the north, Menó-Mené in the centre, Meriaq-Meriku, Ngenó-Ngené and Nggetó-Nggeté.</p><p>Most Sasak follow Lima Waktu ('Five Times') <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, with the five daily prayers that orthodox teachers prescribe, while followers of Wetu Telu ('Three Times') pray three times a day. Large Wetu Telu communities remain, especially in Bayan, where the religion originated. Weaving is an important part of Sasak culture, and the Gandrung and Gendang beleq dances are among their performing arts.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sasak people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A Hindu Balinese elite ruled a majority of Muslim <b>Sasak</b> farmers, taxing their rice and controlling their aristocrats.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-mataram-lombok">Kingdom of Mataram (Lombok)</a></li><li><blockquote>Selaparang was one of several <b>Sasak</b> principalities on Lombok, centred in the east of the island near the coast facing Sumbawa.</blockquote><a href="/state/selaparang-kingdom">Selaparang Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
